def remove_duplicate_emails(users):
    seen = set()
    result = []
    for user in users:
        if user['email'] not in seen:
            seen.add(user['email'])
            result.append(user)
    return result

# Example
users = [
    {'name': 'Alice', 'email': 'alice@example.com'},
    {'name': 'Bob', 'email': 'bob@example.com'},
    {'name': 'Alice2', 'email': 'alice@example.com'},
    {'name': 'Charlie', 'email': 'charlie@example.com'},
    {'name': 'Bob2', 'email': 'bob@example.com'},
]

result = remove_duplicate_emails(users)
# Result: Alice, Bob, Charlie (duplicates removed, first occurrence kept)
```

**JavaScript version:**
```javascript
function removeDuplicateEmails(users) {
    const seen = new Set();
    return users.filter(user => {
        if (seen.has(user.email)) return false;
        seen.add(user.email);
        return true;
    });
}
```

**SQL version (if data is in a database):**
```sql
SELECT DISTINCT ON (email) * FROM users ORDER BY email, id;
```

Tracks seen emails in a set, filters once → O(n) time, preserves order and first occurrence.
